<!-- ================= 4. BACKEND API SPECIFICATIONS ================= -->
<h1>4. Backend API Route Specifications</h1>
<p>
  All backend endpoints are built using Next.js Route Handlers (App Router) under <code>app/api/</code>.
</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Route Endpoint</th>
      <th>HTTP Method</th>
      <th>Auth / Guard</th>
      <th>Description &amp; Business Logic</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><code>/api/checkout</code></td>
      <td>POST</td>
      <td>Customer Session</td>
      <td>Performs atomic oversell verification against warehouse inventory, saves <code>shipping_pincode</code> to customer profile, decrements physical stock, records orders, and returns receipt payload.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>/api/admin/verify</code></td>
      <td>GET</td>
      <td>Bearer JWT</td>
      <td>Validates Supabase auth token using Service Role, inspects user metadata and customer role, and returns <code>{ isAdmin: true/false }</code>.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>/api/admin/update-stock</code></td>
      <td>POST</td>
      <td>Admin Guard</td>
      <td>Updates physical <code>stock_quantity</code> in the warehouse inventory. Automatically updates status to <code>OUT_OF_STOCK</code> if quantity equals zero.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>/api/admin/update-expiry</code></td>
      <td>POST</td>
      <td>Admin Guard</td>
      <td>Modifies the <code>expiry_date</code> column of a specific inventory batch.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>/api/admin/products/delete</code></td>
      <td>POST</td>
      <td>Admin Guard</td>
      <td>Performs cascade deletion: removes inventory records and order item associations before deleting the master product row.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>/api/admin/create-user</code></td>
      <td>POST</td>
      <td>Admin Guard</td>
      <td>Creates a new staff user via Supabase Auth Admin API with pre-verified email and provisions the <code>admin</code> role.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>/api/admin/first-setup</code></td>
      <td>POST</td>
      <td>Setup Key Override</td>
      <td>Bootstrapping route that force-grants administrative privileges to an account using <code>ADMIN_SETUP_KEY</code>.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<!-- ================= 5. KEY ALGORITHMIC MECHANISMS ================= -->
<h1>5. Key Algorithmic Mechanisms</h1>

<h3>5.1 Strict Flash Sale Oversell Protection</h3>
<p>
  During flash sales, hundreds of shoppers may attempt to purchase scarce inventory at the exact same second. FlashCart enforces a two-tier protection mechanism:
</p>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Client-Side Guard:</strong> The shopping cart blocks users from incrementing the requested quantity past the latest fetched stock level.</li>
  <li><strong>Server-Side Atomic Guard:</strong> In <code>/api/checkout/route.js</code>, the server queries the database immediately inside the checkout transaction:
    <pre><code>if (requested &gt; availableStock) {
    return NextResponse.json({
        success: false,
        message: 'Flash Sale Oversell Protection: Only ' + availableStock + ' units remaining!'
    }, { status: 409 });
}</code></pre>
    If valid, the server executes an atomic stock decrement:
    <pre><code>newStock = Math.max(0, availableStock - requested);
UPDATE inventory SET stock_quantity = newStock WHERE product_id = ...;</code></pre>
  </li>
</ol>

<h3>5.2 Date-Based Expiry Classification</h3>
<p>
  Inventory freshness is computed dynamically in JavaScript without requiring recurring background cron jobs:
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Expired Condition:</strong> <code>item.expiry_date &lt; CURRENT_DATE</code> (Flagged in bold red alert).</li>
  <li><strong>Expiring This Week Condition:</strong> <code>item.expiry_date &gt;= CURRENT_DATE &amp;&amp; item.expiry_date &le; CURRENT_DATE + 7 days</code> (Flagged in amber warning).</li>
  <li><strong>Fresh Condition:</strong> <code>item.expiry_date &gt; CURRENT_DATE + 7 days</code> (Flagged in green).</li>
</ul>

<!-- ================= 6. DEPLOYMENT & MAINTENANCE ================= -->
<h1>6. Production Deployment &amp; Continuous Integration Protocol</h1>

<h3>6.1 Production Deployment on Vercel</h3>
<p>
  The project is deployed on Vercel's global edge network. Deployment is connected to GitHub for automated Continuous Integration and Continuous Deployment (CI/CD).
</p>
<ol>
  <li>Create a GitHub repository and push the <code>main</code> branch.</li>
  <li>Import the repository into <a href="https://vercel.com">Vercel</a>.</li>
  <li>Configure Production Environment Variables:
    <ul>
      <li><code>NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL</code></li>
      <li><code>NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY</code></li>
      <li><code>SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY</code></li>
      <li><code>DATABASE_URL</code></li>
      <li><code>ADMIN_SETUP_KEY</code></li>
    </ul>
  </li>
  <li>Click <strong>Deploy</strong>. Vercel automatically compiles the Next.js bundle and publishes the site live.</li>
</ol>

<h3>6.2 Making Live Updates in the Future</h3>
<div class="callout">
  <div class="callout-title">Standard 3-Step Continuous Deployment Cycle</div>
  <p>Once deployed on Vercel, any code change made locally is automatically tested, built, and pushed to the live website by running:</p>
  <pre><code>git add .
git commit -m "Description of changes made"
git push origin main</code></pre>
  <p>Vercel detects the push on branch <code>main</code> and redeploys the live application in ~60 seconds with zero downtime.</p>
</div>

